+++ b/guest-linux/aarch64/docs/uflow-managed-write-v1.md
@@ -0,0 +1,104 @@
+# Catalog W grants and current-domain metadata
+
+The write extension accelerates the existing scalar SDK stores and their native
+verification reads under an authenticated Catalog `Executing W` / `Writing`
+lease. Every byte still originates in a CPU SDK operation. The feature defaults
+off; enabling it does not create a grant. An ungranted callback uses the original
+strict native path. The accepted read-cache codec and wire remain unchanged.
+
+Apply `patches/managed-write-v1/qemu-managed-write-v1.patch` to the accepted
+managed-read source overlay identified by `source-pins.json` using
+`git apply --unidiff-zero`, then copy that
+directory's `files/` tree into the QEMU source tree. The delta changes UBC and its
+managed-read include; the two new files implement the W sidecar and bounded
+staging core. UMMU, coherence, message queue, public UBC layout and SDK sources
+are inherited unchanged. The source pins identify all eleven resulting files.
+
+## Runtime contract
+
+Select the separately validated write profile and enable both explicit globals:
+
+```
+-global ubc.uflow-managed-cache-v1=on
+-global ubc.uflow-managed-write-v1=on
+```
+
+`uflow_managed_guest_group_v2.py` requires `managed_write_profile` containing
+`manifest`, `manifest_sha256`, and `validator_sha256`. The actual selected
+binary, profile validator, wrapper and both codecs must form one verified
+closure. `ManagedSerialChannel.managed_write(wire)` returns an exact typed
+metadata dictionary through the owning QMP peer. This uses the same five-second
+transaction deadline and node lock as `managed_cache`, with PID, birth, UID and
+PID/mount/user namespace checks. One R or W grant can be outstanding per QEMU.
+
+`uflow_managed_write_codec_v1.py` encodes 220 binary bytes as `umw1:` followed by
+440 lowercase hexadecimal characters. The binary layout is
+`!4sB3xQ32s32s32s9Q9I`, magic `UMW1`. Operations are `probe=1`, `begin=2`,
+`end=3`, `status=4`, and `flush=5`. The exact property is
+`/machine/uflow-managed-write-control-v1`.
+
+Commands bind the control generation, authority/correlation/descriptor digests,
+all public native aliases and the exact offset/length. `flush` and `end` must
+match every accepted grant field; only the operation byte may differ. The
+descriptor helper reads public MSOB-v2 slices and hashes the original opaque
+hex. It never decodes or returns the secret at offset 92. Native key flags zero
+and public segment flags seven have different meanings.
+
+The reply adds `uncertain`, `dirty_bytes`, `write_in_flight`, and five canonical
+uint64 decimal-string `write_counters`: `cpu_callbacks`, `accepted_bytes`,
+`strict_flush_calls`, `completed_bytes`, and `failures`. Other native aliases and
+read counters retain their strict typed schemas. Successful begin/flush/end
+require zero dirty bytes, zero write/read IO and no uncertainty/failures. Begin
+starts active and empty. Flush remains active. End is inactive with zero pages.
+
+C owns authority, obligation tracking and the complete sequence:
+`probe → begin → original SDK stores and verification → flush → end → commit`.
+Committed R authority is not W authority. A failed or ambiguous control leaves
+the obligation Unknown; normal channel close and QMP quit remain blocked. The
+wrapper notifies the independent monitor before further failure handling.
+
+## Native data path
+
+One staged 4 KiB page and a 512-byte dirty mask retain only CPU-written bytes.
+Changing pages or reading flushes contiguous dirty runs through the original
+strict native IO implementation. Complete pages flush during the existing
+store loop. Holes and bytes outside the granted range are never synthesized.
+Write verification uses a bounded 8 MiB native read cache under the same W
+grant. Successful end drains at most the current staged page and retires the
+cache; it never replaces native completion with a send-queue acknowledgment.
+
+The full native identity is checked before and after IO. Partial/short/malformed
+ACKs, changed epochs or tokens, failed readback, and dirty or in-flight
+invalidation/revocation make uncertainty sticky. Close/unmap/retire guards retain
+the W obligation. An exact native FENCE flushes its pending dirty bytes. These
+semantics apply to the opted-in Catalog CPU SDK path.
+
+## Evidence APIs
+
+`uflow_managed_qemu_profile_v2.validate(manifest, external_sha, selected_binary)`
+checks the accepted R ancestor, an actual reproduced control ELF, one new UBC
+object, ordered link recipe, unchanged inputs, compiler dependencies, all eleven
+sources, both codecs, wrapper and source-bound HOST results. It grants no boot
+or accounting authority. HOST timing is separate from actual Guest timing.
+
+`uflow_recovery_context_v4.evaluate(context, external_sha)` and
+`validate_candidate(candidate, external_sha)` describe the actual failed c101
+domain: source071/daemon927, image066, observed R profile, original monitor
+ownership, current119/120/121 and the complete prior history. They verify 84
+retained files and 179 original births. The old consumed v3 inventory is derived
+without calling its eligibility predicate. The new W runtime is prospective and
+cannot replace the observed c101 ELF. Unknown and missing normal journals stay
+INCOMPLETE; evidence production does not mutate accounting or acknowledge SDK
+release.
+
+`uflow_profile_metadata_v4.py` retains the v3 API signatures:
+`accepted_context(path, sha)`, `validate_registration(request, sha, helper,
+accepted_path, accepted_sha)`, `historical_inventory(accepted_path, accepted_sha)`,
+and `validate_closed_union(plan, sha, accepted_path, accepted_sha)`. Registration
+still requires both genuine complete journals, matching publication, worker and
+native events, actual selected runtime/capture and real ownership. Append-only
+registry entries use the independent v4 schema and namespace. The historical
+84-file set is combined with every new carrier and normal metadata entry; an
+isolated fixture with ten carriers and two metadata files therefore has 96.
+Quarantined historical markers never become normal registrations. C alone owns
+receipt semantics, claims, fences, accounting transitions and future admission.
